  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 139 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nA complicated pattern will set up early next week. An active \nnorthern stream will undulate across the western and central CONUS \nas multiple disturbances cross the northern tier and southern Canada \nSunday/Monday. By late Monday, a modest longwave trough will cross \nthe central Rockies and eject onto the Plains. Upper-level dynamics \nwill be maximized south of the area as high pressure builds over the \nnorthern Plains. This will establish colder air across the region \nwith lift from the crossing trough leading to low-end precipitation \nchances. The latest PoPs from the model blend (NBM) paints 15-20% \npotential largely for areas south of Highway 2. Given the cold air \nin place, most precipitation should fall as snow but light QPF will \npreclude a greater interruption to the pre-Thanksgiving travel \nperiod. While the EPS/GEFS both suggest minor accumulating snow, \ntheir more aggressive outputs from the 90th percentile fall short of \neven two inches anywhere in the area save for far southwest \nNebraska. Nevertheless, with the cooler temperatures and higher \nvolume of travelers expected, caution is advised to anyone beginning \nthe holiday travel period across the High Plains. Heights quickly \nbuild in by Wednesday, shunting any precipitation to the east and \nallowing dry weather to return. Shortwave ridging should develop \ntowards the middle of next week which will largely support mild \ntemperatures, but differences in said ridging magnitude between \nmajor synoptic models casts some uncertainty. Even so, NBM inner-\nquartile spread remains fairly small around 10F with temperatures \nfavoring the 40s to potentially near 50F for the area. That would \nsuggest a fairly nice holiday with negligible precipitation chances. \nModel difference increase substantially for the following holiday \nweekend, making for a very low confidence mid and extended range \nforecast.",
